30.4 ADS-B Extended Squitter
Automatic Dependent Surveillance–Broadcast (ADS-B) is the friendliest part of the decoding because it is broadcast and self-describing. Aircraft periodically transmit extended-squitter messages on 1090 MHz, usually as DF 17 for transponder-originated messages and DF 18 for some non-transponder devices, e.g. ground vehicles.
The payload begins with a type code (TC) that identifies the content: aircraft identification, airborne position, surface position, airborne velocity, or operational status. In Mode S terminology, the ADS-B message field (ME) is also associated with Comm-B Data Selector-style labels: for example BDS 0,8 for aircraft identification, BDS 0,5 for airborne position, and BDS 0,9 for airborne velocity.
Common type-code groups are:



Table 30.2: Most commonly used Mode S type codes for ADS-B Extended Squitter









Type code
