Caution



We have skipped over a few practical issues in ADS-B decoding. Two caveats are worth keeping in mind:

Decoded fields are not always the same as the raw bits in the frame. For example, latitude and longitude are encoded as compact CPR values (cpr_lat and cpr_lon) and need additional processing before they become ordinary coordinates.
Some decoded floating-point values are displayed with excessive precision. This comes from converting quantities encoded on a small number of bits into floating-point numbers. The actual precision is much lower, so these values can safely be rounded before practical use.

30.5 Compact Position Reporting
ADS-B position messages do not contain latitude and longitude as ordinary decimal coordinates. They use Compact Position Reporting (CPR), a grid encoding designed to fit a useful global position into a small payload. Airborne global decoding needs two recent messages from the same aircraft: one even CPR frame and one odd CPR frame. Local decoding can use a nearby reference position, such as a receiver or airport location.
The intuition is that one CPR frame does not identify a single point globally. It identifies the same fractional position repeated over a grid. An even frame and an odd frame use slightly different grids; when the two are paired, only one neighbourhood is consistent with both reports.

cprToy = ({
  extent: 16,
  point: {x: 9.2, y: 6.7},
  even: {step: 4, origin: {x: 0, y: 0}, label: "even grid", color: "#2f6fbb"},
  odd: {step: 16 / 5, origin: {x: 0, y: 0}, label: "odd grid", color: "#d97904"},
  selected: "#238443",
  grid: "#d8dee4"
})







cprLines = (step, origin = 0, extent = cprToy.extent) =&gt; {
  const values = [];
  for (let v = origin; v &lt;= extent + 1e-9; v += step) values.push(v);
  for (let v = origin - step; v &gt;= -1e-9; v -= step) values.push(v);
  return values.filter((v) =&gt; v &gt;= 0 && v &lt;= extent).sort((a, b) =&gt; a - b);
}







cprCandidates = ({step, origin}, point = cprToy.point, extent = cprToy.extent) =&gt; {
  const mod = (value, base) =&gt; ((value % base) + base) % base;
  const fx = mod(point.x - origin.x, step);
  const fy = mod(point.y - origin.y, step);
  const xs = [];
  const ys = [];
  for (let x = origin.x + fx; x &lt;= extent + 1e-9; x += step) xs.push(x);
  for (let x = origin.x + fx - step; x &gt;= -1e-9; x -= step) xs.push(x);
  for (let y = origin.y + fy; y &lt;= extent + 1e-9; y += step) ys.push(y);
  for (let y = origin.y + fy - step; y &gt;= -1e-9; y -= step) ys.push(y);
  return xs.flatMap((x) =&gt; ys.map((y) =&gt; ({x, y})))
    .filter((d) =&gt; d.x &gt;= 0 && d.x &lt;= extent && d.y &gt;= 0 && d.y &lt;= extent);
}







