31.9 Data caveats
Datalink data is rich but uneven:

coverage depends on receiver location, bearer, propagation, and aggregation network;
labels and envelopes route messages, but airline-specific payloads are often undocumented;
public feeds may include duplicates, partial messages, or rows without a decoded payload;
CPDLC and ADS-C decoding require bit-level application parsers;
privacy and safety concerns are stronger than for simple position broadcasts.

Use datalink as a complementary source: it can explain what the aircraft and ground systems communicated, but it should be interpreted with the same care as any operational communication record.

32.4 Example decoded report
The exact JSON shape depends on the decoder, but a normalized ADS-C event usually exposes the ARINC 622 envelope, the ADS IMI, and the decoded tags. A compact example looks like this:
{
  "bearer": "vdl2",
  "path": "acars",
  "message_class": "app_message",
  "summary": "ADS-C: 48.3420°N 007.8210°W FL350 track 280.3° gs 487kt",
  "app": {
    "protocol": "ads_c",
    "registration": "G-EZBJ",
    "tags": [
      {
        "BasicReport": {
          "latitude": 48.342,
          "longitude": -7.821,
          "altitude_ft": 35000,
          "timestamp_seconds_past_hour": 1843.0,
          "position_accuracy_code": 2,
          "tcas_ok": true
        }
      },
      {
        "PredictedRoute": {
          "next_latitude": 50.0,
          "next_longitude": -15.0,
          "next_altitude_ft": 35000,
          "next_eta_seconds": 1240
        }
      },
      {
        "MeteoData": {
          "wind_speed_kt": 62.0,
          "wind_direction_true_degrees": 245.0,
          "temperature_c": -54.5
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
The PredictedRoute tag is particularly valuable for data analysis: it gives a glimpse of the aircraft’s planned path, not just its current position. The meteorological tag is also useful because it provides in-situ measurements along routes where conventional observations are sparse.

32.5 Uplink contracts
ADS-C uplinks are not just protocol noise. They describe the surveillance contract itself. For example, a periodic contract request may specify an interval and a list of report groups. An event contract may ask the aircraft to report when it crosses a waypoint, deviates laterally, changes altitude, or enters an emergency condition.
A decoder that keeps only downlink positions loses this context. For research, uplinks can explain why reports appear at a given cadence and why some fields are present or absent.
{
  "summary": "ADS-C uplink: periodic contract every 600s, demand basic+flight_id+predicted_route",
  "app": {
    "protocol": "ads_c_uplink",
    "periodic_interval": 600,
    "demand_groups": ["basic", "flight_id", "predicted_route"]
  }
}

32.6 Access paths
ADS-C messages can be observed through several paths:

Airframes.io and similar aggregators may expose ACARS, HFDL, SATCOM, or VDL2-origin rows;
local VHF or VDL2 reception can capture line-of-sight messages;
HFDL and satellite reception extend coverage to oceanic or remote areas;
institutional datasets, including ANSP or OpenSky-derived sources, may provide decoded ADS-C reports directly.

The datalink and ACARS message sources chapter explains the bearer and envelope layers. For ADS-C analysis, the important fields are the contract, report tags, aircraft identifier, report timestamp, and receiver/source metadata.

32.7 Analytical uses
ADS-C is useful when the research question needs more than high-rate ADS-B positions:

reconstructing oceanic trajectories across ADS-B coverage gaps;
studying route intent through predicted waypoints and estimated times;
comparing in-situ wind and temperature with numerical weather models;
understanding surveillance contracts and report cadences;
linking controller-pilot communication, ADS-C reports, and flown trajectories.

A natural case study is an oceanic flight: ADS-B gives high-rate positions near the coasts, ADS-C continues the trajectory across remote airspace, and CPDLC explains some of the clearances and acknowledgements exchanged along the way.

altitude clearances, e.g. CLIMB TO AND MAINTAIN FL350;
route clearances and oceanic track instructions;
contact instructions, e.g. CONTACT EGTT 129.605 MHz;
pilot responses such as WILCO or UNABLE;
downlinked position reports with time, altitude, route, and meteorological fields;
connection management messages.

33.5 Example: contact instruction
This example is adapted from local datalink analysis fixtures. The raw Airframes row contains an ARINC 622 AT1 envelope; the decoder extracts a CPDLC uplink element:
{
  "atsu_address": "FUKJJYA",
  "imi": "AT1",
  "registration": "HL8572",
  "payload": {
    "type": "Cpdlc",
    "data": {
      "uplink": {
        "header": {
          "msg_id": 8,
          "timestamp": "11:43:54"
        },
        "elements": [
          {
            "id": 117,
            "name": "uM117ICAOunitnameFrequency",
            "template": "CONTACT [icaounitname] [frequency]",
            "body": {
              "icao_unit": {
                "facility": {
                  "type": "Designation",
                  "value": "KSFO"
                },
                "function": "Center"
              },
              "frequency": {
                "type": "HfKhz",
                "value": 10048
              }
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}
For a human reader this means a contact instruction. For data analysis it is a structured event with a facility, frequency, aircraft, timestamp, and message direction.

33.6 Example: altitude clearance
A compact altitude-clearance example decodes to a uM20 element:
{
  "atsu_address": "BNECAYA",
  "imi": "AT1",
  "registration": "VH-EBN",
  "payload": {
    "type": "Cpdlc",
    "data": {
      "uplink": {
        "header": {
          "msg_id": 5,
          "msg_ref": 5,
          "timestamp": "11:48:36"
        },
        "elements": [
          {
            "id": 20,
            "name": "uM20Altitude",
            "template": "CLIMB TO AND MAINTAIN [altitude]",
            "body": {
              "altitude": {
                "FL": 350
              }
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}
Such messages are useful when aligned with a trajectory: the climb can be compared with the clearance time, the aircraft state, and the following ADS-B or ADS-C reports.

33.7 Route clearances and position reports
Route-clearance elements can contain structured route information rather than plain text. A route clearance may include departure and destination airports, published identifiers, oceanic waypoints, runways, procedures, or additional route details.
{
  "name": "uM80RouteClearance",
  "body": {
    "route_clearance": {
      "departure": "CYQX",
      "destination": "EGLL",
      "route_information": [
        { "published_identifier": "57N020W" },
        { "published_identifier": "58N030W" },
        { "published_identifier": "MIMKU" }
      ]
    }
  }
}
Downlinked position reports (dM48) can be even richer. They may include current position and time, altitude, next fixes, estimated times, temperature, wind, speed, vertical change, track angle, true heading, and reported waypoint fields. This overlaps with ADS-C in content, but the message belongs to the controller-pilot dialogue rather than to a surveillance contract.

33.8 ATN B1 examples
Continental VDL2 can carry CPDLC through the ATN B1 stack instead of an ACARS/ARINC 622 envelope. In that case the protocol stack itself is informative:
{
  "bearer": "vdl2",
  "path": "atn",
  "protocol_stack": ["vdl2", "avlc", "x25", "clnp", "cotp", "ulcs", "atn_b1", "cpdlc"],
  "summary": "ATN B1 CPDLC: uM117UnitNameFrequency: CONTACT EGTT 129.605 MHz",
  "app": {
    "protocol": "atn_b1_cpdlc",
    "standard": "ATN B1",
    "elements": [
      {
        "id": 117,
        "name": "uM117UnitNameFrequency",
        "template": "CONTACT [unit name] [frequency]",
        "body": {
          "facility": "EGTT",
          "frequency": "129.605 MHz"
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
This is why the general datalink and ACARS message sources chapter keeps the bearer and wrapper layers visible. CPDLC data is not only the decoded phrase; it is also the protocol path, direction, facility, and session context.

33.9 Building operational narratives
CPDLC becomes most useful when joined with other sources. A transatlantic narrative might combine:

ADS-B near departure and arrival, where receiver coverage is dense;
ADS-C periodic and event reports over oceanic airspace;
CPDLC clearances, route changes, contact instructions, WILCO responses, and position reports;
flight plan, route, FIR, and airport data.

Together these sources can explain not only where the aircraft flew, but also what it was cleared to do and when the crew acknowledged the instruction.

33.10 Data caveats
CPDLC is operational communication data. Several caveats matter:

public feeds are incomplete and bearer-dependent;
message timestamps may be receiver or aggregator timestamps, not exact cockpit event times;
some payload bodies require complex ASN.1/UPER parsing and may be only partially decoded;
route-clearance structures have many alternatives and edge cases;
free text and operational messages may contain sensitive information;
the absence of a CPDLC message in a public feed does not mean that no instruction was issued.

Use CPDLC to enrich operational analysis, not as a standalone ground truth unless the source and decoding chain are well understood.

Aeronautical Information Publication (AIP);
AIP supplements and amendments;
Notice To Airmen (NOTAM);
Prefilght Information Bulletin (PIB);
Aeronautical Information Circular (AIC);
digital datasets such as AIXM packages, obstacle data, terrain data, procedure coding, and chart data.

34.5 Aeronautical data objects
Aeronautical information systems describe a large set of objects. The same objects appear in charts, flight plans, navigation databases, ATM systems, and analytical datasets.

34.5.1 Airports and aerodromes
At a macroscopic level an airport or aerodrome is identified by codes and represented by an aerodrome reference point. Detailed airport infrastructure data—runways, taxiways, parking positions, surface geometry, and operational metadata—is discussed in airport and infrastructure data. In aeronautical information, airports also act as anchors for procedures, NOTAM, declared distances, charts, and local rules.


34.5.2 Points and navigation aids
A published point may be a waypoint, a fix, a navigation aid, a boundary point, or another named position used for route and procedure definition. Some points correspond to physical radio navigation aids such as VOR, DME, or NDB. Others are coordinate-defined RNAV waypoints with no physical installation.
Common data fields include:

designator or name;
point type;
latitude and longitude;
associated country, FIR, or aerodrome;
navigation-aid frequency or channel when applicable;
magnetic variation or bearing information where relevant;
validity dates and AIRAC cycle.

The operational role of radio navigation aids is covered in conventional radio navigation, while RNAV/RNP concepts are introduced in satellite navigation.


34.5.3 Routes and route segments
An ATS route is a published structure connecting points through route segments. As data, a route segment normally has a start point, end point, directionality, distance, track, vertical limits, restrictions, and availability conditions. Some route networks are airway-based; others support free-route operations where aircraft file direct segments between allowed points.
Analysts should distinguish:

a published route network;
a filed route string in a flight plan;
an accepted or regulated route after flow-management checks;
the flown trajectory reconstructed from surveillance data.

These objects are related but not interchangeable.


34.5.4 Airspaces
Airspaces are geospatial volumes with lateral boundaries, vertical limits, classification, controlling unit, activation rules, and validity. They include FIRs/UIRs, controlled airspaces, terminal areas, control zones, restricted or danger areas, military areas, sectors, and flow-management regions.
The background chapter on airspace structure explains the operational meaning of FIRs, ACCs, sectors, and route structures. In data terms, the hard parts are geometry, vertical references, activation schedules, naming conventions, and versioning.


34.5.5 Procedures
Procedures connect aerodromes to the route network and support instrument operations:

Standard Instrument Departure (SID) for departures;
Standard Terminal Arrival (STAR) for arrivals;
instrument approaches and missed approaches;
holding procedures;
transitions and runway-specific variants.

A procedure is not only a polyline. It may include altitude constraints, speed constraints, fly-by/fly-over path terminators, course or heading instructions, distance conditions, procedure turns, and equipment requirements. Procedure coding in avionics databases therefore requires more structure than a chart drawing.

34.7 Quality and consistency caveats
Aeronautical data is authoritative, but not automatically analysis-ready:

each object must be interpreted for the correct AIRAC cycle;
coordinates may use different precision, datums, or publication conventions;
vertical limits may reference flight levels, altitude, height above surface, or pressure settings;
text notes can override or qualify tabular fields;
temporary information may appear in NOTAM or SUP AIP rather than base AIP data;
procedure geometry may differ between chart depiction, coded path terminators, and flown tracks;
licensing can restrict redistribution of high-quality navigation data.

When joining aeronautical information with trajectories or flight plans, always keep the effective date, source, cycle, and object identifiers. A route or procedure decoded without its publication context can be misleading.

35.2 Flight-plan content
An ICAO flight plan contains structured fields for flight identification, rules, type of flight, aircraft type, wake turbulence category, equipment, departure aerodrome, departure time, cruising speed, level, route, destination, alternate aerodromes, endurance, persons on board, and other operational information.
For trajectory analysis, the most important fields are usually:

aircraft identification or callsign;
aircraft type and performance-relevant information;
departure and destination aerodromes;
estimated off-block or departure time;
requested speed and cruising level;
route field;
equipment and capability indicators;
alternates and remarks when relevant.

The route field is a compact textual representation of intent. It may combine procedure names, waypoints, ATS routes, direct segments, speed/level changes, and special indicators. It must be interpreted against the aeronautical information cycle effective for the flight date.
Example route fragment:
N0441F340 FISTO5B FISTO UY156 PERIG UT210 TUDRA/N0426F280 UT158 AMB AMB9W
This is not a sequence of coordinates. It is a route expression that must be expanded through the relevant procedure and route definitions.

35.3 From filed route to trajectory
A route string can be expanded into a sequence of published points and segments, but a 4D trajectory requires more information:

aircraft performance model;
expected mass and speed schedule;
wind and temperature assumptions;
altitude constraints and level changes;
route restrictions and availability;
airport and runway assumptions;
flow-management measures.

This is why systems such as EUROCONTROL’s Network Manager maintain trajectory models rather than only storing the submitted flight plan. The model is updated as new information becomes available.

35.7 ALL_FT+
ALL_FT+ is a large Network Manager archive format available through DDR2. Files are typically named by date, for example yyyyMMdd.ALL_FT+, and may contain hundreds of columns depending on version. It is best understood as an internal operational archive exposed for analysis rather than as a clean public data model.
Typical information includes:

flight identifiers and callsign;
departure and destination aerodromes;
aircraft type;
flight-plan and regulation fields;
trajectory point sequence;
timestamps and positions;
flight levels and speeds;
airspace and sector penetrations;
route and restriction information.

The richness is attractive, but documentation and metadata can be sparse. Analysts should build small, explicit extraction layers rather than relying on column positions without version checks.

35.9 Planned versus flown trajectories
Planned and flown trajectories differ for several reasons:

tactical shortcuts or directs;
weather deviations;
departure delay or slot regulation;
runway or procedure changes;
level-off during climb;
flow-management rerouting;
holding, vectoring, or sequencing near arrival;
aircraft performance and wind differences.

A robust analysis should name which comparison is being made: filed route versus flown path, FTFM versus ADS-B, RTFM versus CTFM, or NM demand versus actual traffic. Each comparison answers a different question.






TipUseful joins



Flight-plan and NM data become more useful when joined with other sources: ADS-B or radar trajectories for actual movement, ADS-C for oceanic position and intent, CPDLC for clearances, AIRAC-cycle aeronautical data for route expansion, and weather data for rerouting or performance analysis.

35.10 Access and quality caveats
Flight-plan and NM trajectory data are operational data. Access is often restricted, licensed, or subject to aggregation rules. Even when available, the data requires careful handling:

route strings depend on the correct AIRAC cycle;
identifiers can change between filing, operation, and surveillance;
timestamps may represent planned, calculated, regulated, or observed times;
modeled trajectories depend on assumptions not always visible in the file;
late flight-plan changes can invalidate earlier intent;
surveillance-derived updates can blur the distinction between plan and flown path.

Keep the source, model name, cycle, timestamp semantics, and flight identifier lineage explicit. Without these, intent data can easily be mistaken for observed truth.

36.2 Aerodrome observations and forecasts
METAR reports describe observed weather at aerodromes. They typically include time, wind direction and speed, visibility, runway visual range when relevant, present weather, cloud layers, temperature, dew point, pressure, and recent weather remarks. SPECI reports are special observations issued when conditions change significantly.
TAF reports are aerodrome forecasts. They describe expected wind, visibility, weather, cloud, and significant changes over a forecast period. TAFs are useful for pre-flight context and for studying how expected conditions differ from what actually occurred.
For airport and flight-analysis workflows, METAR and TAF data can help explain:

runway configuration changes driven by wind;
low-visibility operations;
convective or precipitation-driven delays;
approach minima constraints;
diversion risk;
discrepancies between planned and actual arrival times.







NoteMETAR is point data



A METAR is an aerodrome observation, not a complete description of the weather along a route. It is useful near the airport but should not be stretched to explain en-route phenomena without additional data.

36.4 Gridded weather and reanalysis
Numerical weather prediction and reanalysis datasets provide gridded atmospheric fields. Common variables include wind components, temperature, pressure, humidity, geopotential height, cloud variables, precipitation, radiation, and turbulence-related diagnostics.
ECMWF’s fifth generation of Atmospheric Reanalysis (ERA5) is widely used in research because it provides global historical reanalysis with consistent coverage over decades. Other operational forecast products may have higher temporal resolution, lower latency, or better suitability for real-time applications.
For trajectory enrichment, gridded data is usually interpolated in four dimensions: latitude, longitude, altitude or pressure level, and time. This makes it possible to attach wind, temperature, or humidity to each trajectory point.
Important caveats:

model altitude coordinates may be pressure levels, model levels, or height fields;
interpolation choices can affect derived wind and temperature;
convective hazards are often under-resolved in global models;
forecast data and reanalysis data answer different questions;
model fields should not be treated as direct observations.

36.6 Aircraft-derived weather data
Aircraft themselves provide weather observations. Sources include:

AMDAR or Mode-S-derived wind and temperature estimates;
ADS-C meteorological tags;
CPDLC position reports with meteorological fields;
turbulence reports or eddy-dissipation-rate products;
pilot reports where available.

Aircraft-derived data is valuable because it samples the atmosphere along actual flight routes and at cruise altitudes. It is also uneven: coverage follows traffic flows, reporting depends on equipage and data-sharing agreements, and measurements may require calibration or quality control.
The ADS-C data and CPDLC data chapters describe datalink messages that may contain wind, temperature, or position-report information.

36.7 Operational impacts and hazards
Weather influences operations through several mechanisms:

thunderstorms and convection cause rerouting, airborne holding, ground stops, and diversions;
turbulence affects flight-level choice, passenger safety, and route planning;
icing constrains climb/descent and affects aircraft performance;
wind shear and microbursts are critical near airports;
fog and low visibility reduce airport capacity and may trigger low-visibility procedures;
crosswinds and tailwinds influence runway selection and landing limits;
runway contamination affects braking action and take-off/landing performance;
strong winds aloft affect flight time, fuel burn, and route choice.

This operational interpretation should be grounded in the background weather chapter. In data work, the key task is to choose sources that match the phenomenon: METAR for airport visibility, radar or satellite for convection, gridded winds for cruise performance, SIGMET for advisory regions, and aircraft reports for in-situ conditions.

36.8 Environmental data
Environmental analysis often joins trajectories with additional datasets rather than using weather reports directly. Examples include:

fuel-flow and emissions models;
CO₂ and non-CO₂ climate metrics;
contrail formation potential using humidity and temperature fields;
airport noise contours and population exposure layers;
local air-quality measurements or inventories;
surface weather for dispersion or noise-propagation studies.

These datasets have their own assumptions and scales. For example, contrail studies require upper-air humidity and temperature along the trajectory, while airport noise studies require detailed aircraft type, thrust, procedure, runway, and population data.

36.9 Joining weather with aviation data
Weather joins are usually spatio-temporal. Common patterns are:

nearest-station join: attach the closest METAR to airport movements;
validity-window join: match a TAF, SIGMET, or NOTAM-like product by time and area;
grid interpolation: interpolate ERA5 or forecast fields to trajectory points;
polygon intersection: test whether a flight crossed a hazard region;
event-window analysis: compare delays, diversions, or holdings before and after weather onset.

Be explicit about time semantics. Observations, forecasts, advisories, issue times, valid times, and reception times are different. Mixing them can accidentally use future information in a retrospective analysis.

36.10 Data caveats
Weather and environmental data can explain a lot, but they can also mislead:

airport observations do not describe en-route weather;
forecasts should not be evaluated as if they were observations;
global models smooth local hazards;
advisory products reflect both meteorology and operational issuance practices;
aircraft reports are biased toward traffic density;
environmental metrics depend strongly on model assumptions;
time zones, validity periods, and vertical references require careful handling.

For reproducible research, store the data source, version, issue time, validity time, interpolation method, and spatial reference. Weather enrichment should be traceable, especially when it is used to explain delays, safety events, or environmental impact.

